<p>
Most of you know that I think very highly of Álvaro Ramírez. That’s mainly because I’m a <i>huge</i> fan of <a href="https://xenodium.com/journelly-for-ios">Journelly</a>, which I use many times a day as my memo book. It has many other uses so take a look at the link if you’re interested in a note taking/journaling/private tweet/memo book application. I can’t recommend it enough.
</p>
<p>
Ramírez has, of course, done many other things, many or perhaps most, in the Emacs sphere. See his <a href="https://xenodium.com/">site</a> for details. In a recent post he <a href="https://xenodium.com/my-emacs-31-contributions">writes about making contributions to Emacs and getting them merged</a>. Those of you who have been down that road know that it’s hard to get started. You have to navigate the GNU copyright assignment process, which seems needlessly baroque until you remember that all this is being run by volunteers.
</p>
<p>
You can follow the link to find out what his contributions are. They are all live in Emacs 31 so if you are up to date they are available to you now. The main takeaway for me was the satisfaction that Ramírez got from making those contributions. None of them were huge changes and merely scratched an itch that Ramírez had. If you have a small itch that you’ve scratched, you should consider sharing it with the community. You too can become part of Emacs’ long and influential history just as Hari Seldon’s successors in the <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Foundation_universe#Psychohistory">Second Foundation</a> contributed their own additions to Seldon’s Psychohistory.</p>
